{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790338100000-architect-dao-stream-serve-q1-q7-mob-r3.glm-5.3.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 12:18:06 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 19:18:06 +07\n     3\t\n     4\t# dao.stream.serve Q1–Q7 — round 3, final confirmation (GLM-5.3)\n     5\t\n     6\tI read codex's round-2 answer verbatim and re-verified its new citations (`dao.lease.cljc`: `judge-step` at 1433, `make-judge` at 2114, `make-holder` at 2215 — all accurate; its doc citations were verified in rounds 1–2). On the orchestrator's reading: **confirmed for Q3 and Q4, corrected for Q5** — Q5 differs in one small way, not two.\n     7\t\n     8\t---\n     9\t\n    10\t## Q3 — CONFIRMED\n    11\t\n    12\tBoth final positions are, in substance: opaque composition-assigned peer ids in v1, confined to explicitly trusted deployments; a specified duplicate-`:serve/register` rule for the relay (open spec sentence both models list); the public-relay path deferred to real authentication work. Codex r2 adopted my v1 position and conceded the point my round-1 argument turned on (\"signing registration alone does not protect later UDP traffic\"); its remaining nuance — that a public relay wants proof of control at registration *plus* an authenticated channel — is compatible with my formulation (\"channel auth + self-certifying stream identity\"), since a Noise-shaped channel's registration handshake is precisely where a binding proof would live. Its correction of my r1 phrasing (\"every frame must be individually signed… too strong for an authenticated channel\") is fair and already reflected in my r2 wording (\"channel authentication\"). The one citation caveat (\"the discovery text describes *stream* kickoff identities, not a peer-registration protocol\") is true and cuts no ice either way — both of us use it only as the additive-rule precedent the design itself cites (findings.md:284). Nothing substantive remains. Residual work item (agreed, not a split): write the duplicate-registration sentence into serve §7.3.\n    13\t\n    14\t## Q4 — CONFIRMED\n    15\t\n    16\tSubstance identical on all four elements: relay admission is composition-supplied policy; open admission is legitimate only where the deployment explicitly trusts its peers (dev/trusted v1 default); link/peer-table lifecycle and capacity bounds are required under either policy (codex adopted my flag verbatim: \"those are needed under either policy\"); postage is not the relay-admission answer now (`dao.stream.discovery.md:231-233`), and the contract's no-gating rule governs descriptor attachment, not relay routing state (`dao.stream.md:285-292`; `register`/`dial` are serve-layer frames, findings.md:43). Codex's table marks \"Agrees with GLM: no\" — a bookkeeping artifact of *who moved toward whom* (codex held its r1 position; I changed to it in r2), not a substantive difference; its body adopts the same rule my r2 states. Residual work items (agreed): the admission-seam + bounded-state sentences in serve §7.3; public-relay policy deferred.\n    17\t\n    18\t## Q5 — DIFFERS (one item, not two)\n    19\t\n    20\t**(ii) OD-1 revise-vs-accept — converged in substance.** Both positions now require the same corrected rule: an unrecognized **write** outcome permits *no inference that nothing was appended*, and is not retried unless `:dao.stream/retry? true` (reads keep \"nothing observed\"). Codex r2: \"Adopt OD-1's goal **now**, but revise its proposed fallback before acceptance\" — since the OD process replaces the entry with the amended text on acceptance (`dao.stream.md:796-801`), \"revise the fallback before acceptance\" and \"accept with the revision folded in\" are the same event with the commit ordering swapped; no design content differs. To the extent codex means acceptance is *delayed* past the implementation precondition, the evidence decides for accepting the corrected rule with the bundle: current consumers throw on any unrecognized outcome (`engine.cljc:41-43`; `handle-put` default → fail at 192-195; `handle-next` default → fail at 245-254; doc record `dao.stream.md:818-821`), serve relays source outcomes verbatim (findings.md:103), so there is no safe intermediate period — and codex's own claim-(b) verdict concedes \"those facts make an unknown-outcome policy important.\" I treat (ii) as settled between us: OD-2 and OD-3(a)+(2) accepted as drafted; OD-1 accepted with the corrected write fallback, as a precondition of serve/UCF implementation.\n    21\t\n    22\t**(i) Proxy cached-read semantics — still differs; I hold.** Codex r2 keeps \"truthful proxy outcomes\" / \"repair proxy read semantics\" as a separate implementation gate; I hold that the declaration path is the only implementable resolution, on evidence that decides it:\n    23\t\n    24\t1. An immediate answer that is truthful **against the source** is unobtainable: it requires a synchronous consultation, which the contract forbids (\"No operation waits… an operation that cannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is called, and the eventual answer arrives… as data\", `dao.stream.md:153-158`, also 81-82). The contract already blesses deferred remote truth for `attach!` and `append!` (`dao.stream.md:301-302`, 451-453); blocked-on-miss is the same pattern for reads.\n    25\t2. The only alternative — a new \"fetching/unknown\" outcome — crashes every current consumer, because unrecognized outcomes throw (`engine.cljc:41-43`, 192-195, 245-254). A new outcome is safe **only under OD-1's rule**, i.e., the gate codex proposes to place *before* OD acceptance is in fact gated *by* it. The ordering is backwards.\n    26\t3. The properties that make cached reads safe are already pinned as acceptance tests: gap fidelity and no-skip anchors (serve criteria 2 and 3, findings.md:289-290), plus \"never produces a `gap` the source did not\" (findings.md:154). Those tests are the implementation gate; nothing else is needed.\n    27\t\n    28\tMy final position on (i), refined to absorb codex's legitimate concern that a subordinate spec cannot legalize what the contract forbids (\"Where the two disagree, the contract wins\", findings.md:6): **a declarative paragraph in serve §4 *plus* one clarifying contract sentence riding with the OD acceptances** (natural home: alongside the OD-3(2) cursor-serialization text, `dao.stream.md:939-943`) stating that a handle may answer from its observed state — blocked for not-yet-fetched reads, stale-but-never-skipping anchors — with `gap`/`end` only ever relayed. That gives the semantics contract-level blessing without a new outcome and without gating on a rewrite cycle. If codex accepts declaration-with-contract-sentence, Q5 closes; otherwise (i) goes to the owner with my advice: take the declaration path, because the repair path is not implementable under the contract's own no-waiting rule.\n    29\t\n    30\t---\n    31\t\n    32\tCorrection to the orchestrator's note, for the record: Q3 and Q4 do match in substance (confirmed above); Q5 differs in **one** way — item (i) only. The OD dispositions inside Q5 (accept OD-2, OD-3(a)+(2), and OD-1-with-corrected-write-fallback) are agreed by both models and can be accepted by the owner regardless of how (i) is ruled.\n    33\t\n    34\tCONSENSUS: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q6, Q7; OPEN: Q5\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
