{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790338100000-architect-dao-stream-serve-q1-q7-mob-r3.gpt-6-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 12:20:12 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 19:20:12 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     3\t\n     4\t**Q3 — CONFIRMED.** Both positions choose opaque, composition-assigned peer IDs for trusted v1 and require a defined duplicate-registration rule. Public deployment remains a separate authentication decision. A peer ID must remain distinct from the logical-stream identity, which the current contract does not authenticate. [Fable design:226](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:226), [Fable design:284](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:284), [dao.stream.md:1087](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:1087)\n     5\t\n     6\t**Q4 — CONFIRMED.** Both positions make relay admission composition-supplied, allow open admission in explicitly trusted deployments, require bounded peer/link state, and leave postage as a possible later policy. The contract’s attachment rule does not decide relay-control admission. [dao.stream.md:285](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:285), [Fable design:226](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:226)\n     7\t\n     8\t**Q5 — DIFFERS on (i); confirmed on (ii).**\n     9\t\n    10\t- **(i) Serve design gate:** A §4 declaration alone cannot make `blocked` on every cache miss mean “nothing at this position yet,” or make a previously observed anchor the current `:newest`. The proxy promises access to the original positioned sequence, while its window is expressly a cache. This needs a contract-consistent resolution before the serve design or implementation is accepted. It **does not gate acceptance of OD-1/2/3**, which answer separate contract questions. [dao.stream.md:421](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:421), [dao.stream.md:477](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:477), [dao.stream.md:542](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:542), [Fable design:125](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:125), [Fable design:137](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:137)\n    11\t- **(ii) OD-1 timing:** Accept **the revised rule now**, in the same decision that corrects its write fallback to “effect unknown; no automatic retry.” “Revise then accept” and “accept with revision” have the same required end state. The draft’s “nothing appended” conflicts with OD-2. [dao.stream.md:796](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:796), [dao.stream.md:836](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:836), [dao.stream.md:879](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:879)\n    12\t\n    13\tCONSENSUS: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q6, Q7; OPEN: Q5\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '69,85p;141,158p;473,500p;536,553p;796,845p;921,947p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '69,85p;141,158p;473,500p;536,553p;796,845p;921,947p'\"","aggregated_output":"    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n   141\t**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is\n   142\ttrue now, including the answer \"nothing yet, ask again.\" Nothing parks and\n   143\tnothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.\n   144\tThis is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.\n   145\t\n   146\t`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream\n   147\thas a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this\n   148\tposition holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same\n   149\tanswer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a\n   150\tcallback; the Result Convention's \"never exceptions and never callbacks\" is\n   151\tthis model stated as a rule about results.\n   152\t\n   153\tThe rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that\n   154\tcannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is\n   155\tcalled, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as\n   156\tdata, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A\n   157\tDaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be\n   158\treported back. It is a question about now.\n   473\t## Cursors\n   474\t\n   475\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   476\t\n   477\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   478\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   479\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   480\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   481\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   482\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   483\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   484\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   485\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   486\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n   487\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   488\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   489\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   490\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   491\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   492\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   493\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   494\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   495\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   496\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   497\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   498\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   499\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   500\t- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n   536\t## Reading\n   537\t\n   538\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   539\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   540\tanswer.\n   541\t\n   542\t| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |\n   543\t|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n   544\t| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n   545\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   546\t| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n   547\t| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n   548\t| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n   549\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n   550\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the read; nothing was observed.               | —                                                                                       |\n   551\t\n   552\tReading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same\n   553\tposition observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the\n   796\tStatus: **proposed, binding nothing.** Every other section of this document is\n   797\tcontract; this one is not. Each entry names an assumption the contract makes\n   798\twithout stating it, the use that would break it, and a proposed resolution.\n   799\tAn entry leaves this section in one of two ways: accepted, its text moves into\n   800\tthe section it amends and the entry is deleted; or rejected, the entry is\n   801\treplaced by one sentence in *Explicitly Absent* recording why. Drafted\n   802\t2026-09-19 from a review of this document against `dao.stream`, its three\n   803\ttransports (`ringbuffer`, `memory-log`, `ws`), and their consumers.\n   804\t\n   805\tOD-1 to OD-4 are ordered by cost of delay. Each is a paragraph today, while\n   806\tthree transports and a handful of consumers exist, and a migration once a\n   807\tdurable or replicated transport ships. OD-5 was added afterwards and is\n   808\tnumbered by arrival; it concerns the layers above this contract rather than the\n   809\tcontract's own operations.\n   810\t\n   811\t### OD-1. An unrecognized outcome has no defined meaning\n   812\t\n   813\t**The assumption.** The Result Convention makes each operation's outcome set\n   814\texhaustive and makes result *maps* open. The two rules are asymmetric: a future\n   815\textension may add a key, never an outcome. The contract assumes the seven\n   816\toutcome sets are complete for every transport that will ever exist.\n   817\t\n   818\t**Evidence.** Consumers are already total over the closed sets and treat\n   819\tanything else as a fault: `dao.stream.observe/step` classifies an unrecognized\n   820\tread or effect outcome as `transport-error` and reports `:defect` or `:failed`;\n   821\t`yin.vm.engine/handle-put` and `handle-next` throw. *Envelopes* declines to\n   822\treserve an outcome for gating and says gating \"arrives as an addition\", but\n   823\tunder the exhaustive rule a new outcome is not an addition.\n   824\t\n   825\t**What breaks.** Any condition the present outcomes cannot express honestly:\n   826\tattachment or append refused for lack of authority; a descriptor that is valid\n   827\tbut served elsewhere now; a transport that is throttling rather than full; an\n   828\tappend whose effect is unknown (OD-2). Each must either be squeezed into an\n   829\texisting outcome that misdescribes it (`not-found` for unauthorized, `full` for\n   830\tthrottled) or break every consumer.\n   831\t\n   832\t**Proposed resolution.** Keep each outcome set exhaustive *for this version of\n   833\tthe contract* and add one rule to the Result Convention that makes later\n   834\toutcomes safe to introduce:\n   835\t\n   836\t> A consumer that receives an outcome it does not recognize treats it as: not\n   837\t> `ok`; nothing observed and nothing appended; and not retryable, unless the\n   838\t> result map carries `:dao.stream/retry? true`. It does not throw, because an\n   839\t> unrecognized outcome is a newer contract, not a defect in this host's\n   840\t> assembly.\n   841\t\n   842\tWith that rule a newer transport degrades to \"refused, do not retry\" on an\n   843\tolder consumer instead of to an exception, and `:dao.stream/retry?` lets it\n   844\tsay when waiting is the right response. The existing `full` and `blocked` keep\n   845\ttheir meanings and need not carry the key.\n   921\t**Proposed resolution.** Two decisions, the first a choice:\n   922\t\n   923\t1. **Choose what a logical identity ranges over.** Either\n   924\t   (a) *one origin, one transport*: a logical stream is the sequence one\n   925\t   transport instance owns. A copy on another host or medium is a different\n   926\t   logical stream with its own identity; that it mirrors the first is a fact for an\n   927\t   interpreter to record. This is how the code already behaves:\n   928\t   `dao.stream.forward` copies into a destination with its own identity. The\n   929\t   sentence\n   930\t   about different descriptors reaching one stream narrows to \"different\n   931\t   endpoints of the same owning transport\". Or\n   932\t   (b) *contract-level position*: the contract defines a position within an\n   933\t   identity (an ordinal), cursors carry it under a `:dao.stream/…` key, and any\n   934\t   transport serving that identity honors it.\n   935\t   Recommended: (a). It matches Axiom 2 (sameness between sequences is an\n   936\t   interpretation), costs a paragraph, and leaves (b) available later as an\n   937\t   additive key. (b) commits every future transport, including ones with no\n   938\t   natural ordinal, now.\n   939\t2. **Settle serialization.** Replace \"TBD\" with: \"A cursor is plain data and\n   940\t   survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the same\n   941\t   rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names yields\n   942\t   `not-found` at `attach!` or `cursor-mismatch` at `next`, never a defect.\"\n   943\t   Both existing transports already satisfy this.\n   944\t\n   945\t**Cost if deferred.** The first durable or replicated transport decides both\n   946\tquestions implicitly, by whatever its cursor happens to look like, and every\n   947\tconsumer that persisted a cursor inherits that accident.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"The cited lines support the no-waiting constraint and show that a new outcome would reach handlers that currently throw. They do not make a cache miss equivalent to an empty source position. I’m drafting the smallest contract wording I would accept using the existing outcomes."}}
